%I A401036 #9 Nov 03 2026 16:04:29
%S A401036 1,4,10,12,22,26,18,34
%N A401036 Number of orbits of exponents d, 1 <= d <= 2^(2*n+1)-2, under multiplication by 2 modulo 2^(2*n+1)-1, such that the power map x -> x^d on GF(2^(2*n+1)) is almost bent.
%C A401036 Let N = 2*n+1. The power map x^d is almost bent on GF(2^N) if |Sum_{x in GF(2^N)} (-1)^Tr(b*x^d + a*x)| <= 2^((N+1)/2) for all a in GF(2^N) and all b != 0 in GF(2^N), where Tr is the absolute trace from GF(2^N) to GF(2). Every almost bent function is almost perfect nonlinear.
%F A401036 Conjecture: a(n) = 2*phi(2*n+1) + 2 for n >= 5, where phi is A000010. This counts the orbits of the known almost bent exponents and their inverses.
%e A401036 a(1) = 1: for N = 3, the exponents 3, 5, 6 form one orbit modulo 7, and x^3 is almost bent on GF(8).
%o A401036 (PARI) a(k) = {my(n=2*k+1, M=2^n-1, g=ffprimroot(ffgen(2^n, 'z)), X=vector(M, i, g^i), c=0, e, ok, s, h, u, v, mx, y, ix); for(d=1, M-1, if(gcd(d, M)>1, next); e=d; ok=1; for(i=1, n-1, e=2*e%M; if(e<d, ok=0; break)); if(!ok, next); s=vector(2^n, i, 0); s[1]=1; for(i=1, M, y=X[i]; ix=subst(lift(y.pol), 'z, 2); s[ix+1]=1-2*lift(trace(y^d))); h=1; while(h<2^n, for(i=0, 2^n-1, if(bitand(i, h)==0, u=s[i+1]; v=s[i+1+h]; s[i+1]=u+v; s[i+1+h]=u-v)); h*=2); mx=vecmax(vector(2^n, i, abs(s[i]))); if(mx<=2^((n+1)/2), c++)); c}
%Y A401036 Cf. A400802.
%K A401036 nonn,more,new
%O A401036 1,2
%A A401036 _Sajid Khan Hussain_, Oct 14 2026
